## 3. Across context

{{> p10_context }}

**The corpus does reward long context, modestly.** The full cache's perplexity at
{{ phase10.analysis.summary.shortest_context | int }} tokens is
{{ phase10.analysis.summary.full_ppl_ratio_shortest_over_longest | .3f }}× its perplexity at
{{ phase10.analysis.summary.longest_context | int }} on the same scored tokens (95% CI
{{ phase10.analysis.summary.full_ppl_ratio_shortest_over_longest_ci95.0 | .3f }}–{{ phase10.analysis.summary.full_ppl_ratio_shortest_over_longest_ci95.1 | .3f }};
{{ phase10.analysis.summary.full_windows_worse_shortest | int }} of
{{ phase10.analysis.summary.windows | int }} windows worse). Beyond that first step it is not monotone
(the table has 16K reading worse than 8K), so no trend between 8K and 32K is claimed. That matters for reading everything below: if more
context barely helps the reference, removing context can barely hurt a policy, which is §1's
finding seen from the other side.

**A fixed budget fraction costs less perplexity as the context grows — the opposite of what
retrieval does.** Of the {{ phase10.analysis.summary.cost_resolved_at_shortest | int }} conditions
whose cost is resolved at {{ phase10.analysis.summary.shortest_context | int }} tokens, **all
{{ phase10.analysis.summary.cost_falls_with_context | int }}** cost less at
{{ phase10.analysis.summary.longest_context | int }}. The mechanism is the obvious one once seen:
most tokens are predicted from the text just before them, and 25% of 32K is still 8K tokens of
recent text, while 25% of 4K is 1K.

Phase 8's collapse test puts a number on it. Grouping each policy's NLL delta across contexts by the
budget fraction leaves a mean spread of
{{ phase10.analysis.summary.collapse_mean_spread_by_budget_nats | .3f }} nats; grouping it by the
blocks kept leaves {{ phase10.analysis.summary.collapse_mean_spread_by_k_nats | .3f }}, so the blocks
kept hold perplexity {{ phase10.analysis.summary.collapse_fraction_over_k | .2f }}× tighter. For
NIAH retrieval, [Phase 8](PHASE_8.md) found the reverse, with the fraction
{{ phase8.analysis.summary.collapse_k_over_fraction | .1f }}× tighter. Taken alone this collapse is
weak evidence: both spreads are only two to two and a half times the median half-width of a single
condition's interval ({{ phase10.analysis.summary.median_delta_ci_halfwidth_nats | .3f }} nats). The
every-condition trend above is the stronger evidence, and the two point the same way.

**So the two quality metrics disagree about scaling, not only about size.** A residency policy's
retrieval cost is a function of the share of the document it keeps; its perplexity cost is closer
to a function of how much recent text it keeps. A perplexity result measured at one context says
little about retrieval at that context, and less at any other.

